window.docContents[49].resolve({"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Lake/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Build-Tools-and-Distribution--Lake--Caching-Builds--GitHub-Release-Builds--Downloading":{"contents":"To download artifacts, one should configure the package options releaseRepo and buildArchive to point to the GitHub repository hosting the release and the correct artifact name within it (if the defaults are not sufficient).\nThen, set preferReleaseBuild := true to tell Lake to fetch and unpack it as an extra package dependency.\n\nLake will only fetch release builds as part of its standard build process if the package wanting it is a dependency (as the root package is expected to modified and thus not often compatible with this scheme).\nHowever, should one wish to fetch a release for a root package (e.g., after cloning the release's source but before editing), one can manually do so via lake build :release.\n\nLake internally uses curl to download the release and tar to unpack it, so the end user must have both tools installed in order to use this feature.\nIf Lake fails to fetch a release for any reason, it will move on to building from the source.\nThis mechanism is not technically limited to GitHub: any Git host that uses the same URL scheme works as well.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Build Tools\u0009Lake\u0009Caching Builds\u0009GitHub Release Builds","header":"25.1.3.3.1. Downloading","id":"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Lake/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Build-Tools-and-Distribution--Lake--Caching-Builds--GitHub-Release-Builds--Downloading"},"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Lake/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Build-Tools-and-Distribution--Lake--Command-Line-Interface--Building-and-Running":{"contents":"\n\nBuilds the specified facets of the specified targets.Each of the targets is specified by a string of the form:The optional @ and + markers can be used to disambiguate packages and modules from file paths as well as executables, and libraries, which are specified by name as target.\nIf not provided, package defaults to the workspace's root package.\nIf the same target name exists in multiple packages in the workspace, then the first occurrence of the target name found in a topological sort of the package dependency graph is selected.\nModule targets may also be specified by their filename, with an optional facet after a colon.The available facets depend on whether a package, library, executable, or module is to be built.\nThey are listed in the section on facets.When using the local artifact cache, the -o option saves a mappings file that tracks the inputs and outputs of each step in the build.\nThe mappings file describes the targets from one package that are included in the build, restricted to the root package by default.\nTargets that were already up to date are included in the mappings file.\nThe --package option causes the named package's targets to be written to the mappings file instead of the root package's targets, which makes it possible to upload build outputs for a dependency.\nTargets that are not part of the build are not tracked.\nThis mappings file can be used with cache get and cache put to interact with a remote cache.\nThe mappings file is in JSON Lines format, with one valid JSON object per line, and its filename extension is conventionally .jsonl.\n\nTarget and Facet Specifications* a* The default facet(s) of target a\n* @a* The default targets of package a\n* +A* The compilation artifacts of module A (because the default facet of modules is irArts)\n* @a/b* The default facet of target b of package a\n* @a/+A:c* The C file compiled from module A of package a\n* :foo* The root package's facet foo\n* A/B/C.lean:o* The compiled object code for the module in the file A/B/C.lean\n\n\n\nExits with code 0 if the workspace's root package has any default targets configured.\nErrors (with exit code 1) otherwise.check-build does not verify that the configured default targets are valid.\nIt merely verifies that at least one is specified.\n\n\n\n\n\nBuilds a set of targets, reporting progress on standard error and outputting the results on standard out.\nTarget results are output in the same order they are listed and end with a newline.\nIf --json is set, results are formatted as JSON.\nOtherwise, they are printed as raw strings.Targets which do not have output configured will be printed as an empty string or null.\nFor executable targets, the output is the path to the built executable.Targets are specified using the same syntax as in build.\n\nLooks for the executable target exe-target in the workspace, builds it if it is out of date, and then runs\nit with the given args in Lake's environment.See build for the syntax of target specifications and env for a description of how the environment is set up.\n\n\n\nIf no package is specified, deletes the build directories of every package in the workspace.\nOtherwise, it just deletes those of the specified packages.\n\n\n\nWhen cmd is provided, it is executed in the Lake environment with arguments args.If cmd is not provided, Lake prints the environment in which it runs tools.\nThis environment is system-specific.\n\n\n\nBuilds the imports of the given file and then runs lean on it using the workspace's root package's additional Lean arguments and the given args, in that order.\nThe lean process is executed in Lake's environment.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Build Tools\u0009Lake\u0009Command-Line Interface","header":"25.1.5.6. Building and Running","id":"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Lake/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Build-Tools-and-Distribution--Lake--Command-Line-Interface--Building-and-Running"},"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Lake/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Build-Tools-and-Distribution--Lake--Command-Line-Interface--Development-Tools--Tests-and-Linters":{"contents":"\n\nTest the workspace's root package using its configured test driver.A test driver that is an executable will be built and then run with the package configuration's testDriverArgs plus the CLI args.\nA test driver that is a Lake script is run with the same arguments as an executable test driver.\nA library test driver will just be built; it is expected that tests are implemented such that failures cause the build to fail via elaboration-time errors.\n\n\n\nBy default, lint the workspace's root package using its configured lint driver.\nIf builtinLint is set to true in the package configuration, builtin lints also run.Positional module arguments narrow only the builtin lints; if omitted, the workspace's default target roots are used.\nThe lint driver is invoked with lintDriverArgs from the package config plus any arguments after --; the module list is not passed to it.Builtin linting builds the targeted modules with the requested linter options enabled.\nIt is triggered by --builtin-lint, --builtin-only, --linters, or --lint-only, as well as by setting builtinLint to true in the package configuration.\nBy contrast, running the lint driver does not automatically trigger a build of anything but the lint driver itself.The set of environment linters to be run on a declaration is determined by the linter options that were in effect when that declaration was built, whether they were set by set_option in the source or on the command line.\nBoth --linters and --lint-only override those options for the lint build.The options may be:--builtin-lint\n\nRun builtin environment and text linters.\n\n--builtin-only\n\nRun only builtin linters, skipping the lint driver.\n\n--linters <spec>\n\nOverride linter options for the lint build.\nThe <spec> is a comma-separated list of linter option names, each optionally prefixed with - to disable it.\nA name that begins with . is shorthand for the linter. prefix, so that .foo means linter.foo, as in --linters=.foo,-linter.bar.\nThis option may be repeated; for a given linter, later entries override earlier ones.\n\n--lint-only <spec>\n\nLike --linters, but report only the linters that <spec> positively enables, suppressing every other linter, including default-on linters that are not named.\nlinter.all and linter sets are expanded.\nSwitching between --linters and --lint-only replaces the prior spec.\n\n--record-exceptions\n\nRecord each linter warning as a set_option <linter> false in exception by editing the offending source files in place, silencing the warning for that declaration.\nThis implies --builtin-lint.\n\nA lint driver can be configured by either setting the lintDriver package configuration option or by tagging a script or executable with the @[lint_driver] attribute.\nA definition in a dependency can be used as a lint driver by using the <pkg>/<name> syntax for the lintDriver configuration option.A script lint driver will be run with the package configuration's lintDriverArgs plus the CLI args.\nAn executable lint driver will be built and then run like a script.\n\n\n\nCheck if there is a properly configured test driverExits with code 0 if the workspace's root package has a properly\nconfigured lint driver. Errors (with code 1) otherwise.Does NOT verify that the configured test driver actually exists in the\npackage or its dependencies. It merely verifies that one is specified.This is useful for distinguishing between failing tests and incorrectly configured packages.\n\n\n\nCheck if there is a properly configured lint driverExits with code 0 if the workspace's root package has a properly\nconfigured lint driver. Errors (with code 1) otherwise.Does NOT verify that the configured lint driver actually exists in the\npackage or its dependencies. It merely verifies that one is specified.This is useful for distinguishing between failing lints and incorrectly configured packages.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Build Tools\u0009Lake\u0009Command-Line Interface\u0009Development Tools","header":"25.1.5.9.1. Tests and Linters","id":"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Lake/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Build-Tools-and-Distribution--Lake--Command-Line-Interface--Development-Tools--Tests-and-Linters"},"/Functors___-Monads-and--do--Notation/Varieties-of-Monads/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Functors___-Monads-and--do--Notation--Varieties-of-Monads--Exceptions--___Finally___-Computations":{"contents":"Monads that provide the ability to ensure an action happens, regardless of exceptions or other\nfailures.MonadFinally.tryFinally' is used to desugar try ... finally ... syntax.Runs an action, ensuring that some other action always happens afterward.More specifically, tryFinally' x f runs x and then the “finally” computation f. If x\nsucceeds with some value a : α, f (some a) is returned. If x fails for m's definition of\nfailure, f none is returned.tryFinally' can be thought of as performing the same role as a finally block in an imperative\nprogramming language.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Functors, Monads and  do -Notation\u0009Varieties of Monads\u0009Exceptions","header":"19.5.7.3. “Finally” Computations","id":"/Functors___-Monads-and--do--Notation/Varieties-of-Monads/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Functors___-Monads-and--do--Notation--Varieties-of-Monads--Exceptions--___Finally___-Computations"},"/Linters-and-Code-Quality/Linting-Workflows/#env-linters-imports":{"contents":"Environment linters typically consist of two modules.\nOne module defines the linter's option, and it must be imported by every module that will be checked with the linter.\nModules that don't transitively import the linter's option are not checked.\nThis allows the modules to override the linter option's value as needed.\nThe other module defines the linter itself; this module only needs to be imported publicly by the root module from which linting is initiated.\nThe environment linter runs only when it is transitively imported by the root module, but it can be applied to code in modules that the linter itself imports.\nOnly modules that share a prefix with the root module are linted.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Linters\u0009Linting Workflows","header":"26.2.3. Environment Linters and the Import Hierarchy","id":"/Linters-and-Code-Quality/Linting-Workflows/#env-linters-imports"},"/Namespaces-and-Sections/#section-variables":{"contents":"Section variables are parameters that are automatically added to declarations that mention them.\nThis occurs whether or not the option autoImplicit is true.\nSection variables may be implicit, strict implicit, or explicit; instance implicit section variables are treated specially.\n\nWhen the name of a section variable is encountered in a non-theorem declaration, it is added as a parameter.\nAny instance implicit section variables that mention the variable are also added.\nIf any of the variables that were added depend on other variables, then those variables are added as well; this process is iterated until no more dependencies remain.\nAll section variables are added in the order in which they are declared, before all other parameters.\nSection variables are added only when they occur in the statement of a theorem.\nOtherwise, modifying the proof of a theorem could change its statement if the proof term made use of a section variable.\n\nSection variables are not added as definition parameters until after the definition's body has been elaborated.\nThis means that they cannot vary in recursive definitions, and their values are fixed.\nExplicit parameters may shadow section variables and can be used for definitions in which the value must vary.\n\nVariables are declared using the variable command.\n\nVariable Declarations\n\nThe bracketed binders allowed after variable match the syntax used in definition headers.\n\nSection VariablesIn this section, automatic implicit parameters are disabled, but a number of section variables are defined.section\nset_option autoImplicit false\nuniverse u\nvariable {α : Type u} (xs : List α) [Zero α] [Add α]\nBecause automatic implicit parameters are disabled and β is neither a section variable nor bound as a parameter of the function, the following definition fails:def addAll (lst : List β) : β :=\n  lst.foldr (init := 0) (· + ·)\nUnknown identifier `β`\n\nNote: It is not possible to treat `β` as an implicitly bound variable here because the `autoImplicit` option is set to `false`.\nOn the other hand, not even xs needs to be written directly in the definition when it uses the section variables:def addAll :=\n  xs.foldr (init := 0) (· + ·)\n\n\nSection Variables and RecursionSection variables are fixed in recursive functions.\nThe variable length represents a number that should decrease:variable (length : Nat)\nHowever, it cannot be used directly to define recursive functions, because the body of the function does not treat the section variable as a parameter:def copies (x : α) : List α :=\n  match length with\n  | 0 => []\n  | length' + 1 => x :: copies length' x\nThe error arises because copies expects only one explicit argument but has received two:Function expected at\n  copies length'\nbut this term has type\n  List Nat\n\nNote: Expected a function because this term is being applied to the argument\n  x\nEven though length is already a section variable, it can be shadowed in order to define the function:def copies (length : Nat) (x : α) : List α :=\n  match length with\n  | 0 => []\n  | length' + 1 => x :: copies length' x\n\n\nTo add a section variable to a theorem even if it is not explicitly mentioned in the statement, mark the variable with the include command.\nAll variables marked for inclusion are added to all theorems.\nThe omit command removes the inclusion mark from a variable; it's typically a good idea to use it with in.\n\n\n\nIncluded and Omitted Section VariablesThis section's variables include a predicate as well as everything needed to prove that it holds universally, along with a useless extra assumption.section\nvariable {p : Nat → Prop}\nvariable (pZero : p 0) (pStep : ∀ n, p n → p (n + 1))\nvariable (pFifteen : p 15)\nHowever, only p is added to this theorem's assumptions, so it cannot be proved.theorem p_all : ∀ n, p n := by\n  intro n\n  induction n\nThe include command causes the additional assumptions to be added unconditionally:include pZero pStep pFifteen\n\ntheorem p_all : ∀ n, p n := by\n  intro n\n  induction n <;> simp [*]\nBecause the spurious assumption pFifteen was inserted, Lean issues a warning:automatically included section variable(s) unused in theorem `p_all`:\n  pFifteen\nconsider restructuring your `variable` declarations so that the variables are not in scope or explicitly omit them:\n  omit pFifteen in theorem ...\n\nNote: This linter can be disabled with `set_option linter.unusedSectionVars false`\nThis can be avoided by using omit to remove pFifteen:include pZero pStep pFifteen\n\nomit pFifteen in\ntheorem p_all : ∀ n, p n := by\n  intro n\n  induction n <;> simp [*]\nend\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Namespaces and Sections\u0009Section Scopes","header":"6.2.2. Section Variables","id":"/Namespaces-and-Sections/#section-variables"},"/The-Type-System/Inductive-Types/#mutual-inductive-types":{"contents":"Inductive types may be mutually recursive.\nMutually recursive definitions of inductive types are specified by defining the types in a mutual ... end block.\n\nMutually Defined Inductive TypesThe type EvenOddList in a prior example used a Boolean index to select whether the list in question should have an even or odd number of elements.\nThis distinction can also be expressed by the choice of one of two mutually inductive types EvenList and OddList:mutual\n  inductive EvenList (α : Type u) : Type u where\n    | nil : EvenList α\n    | cons : α → OddList α → EvenList α\n  inductive OddList (α : Type u) : Type u where\n    | cons : α → EvenList α → OddList α\nend\n\nexample : EvenList String := .cons \"x\" (.cons \"y\" .nil)\nexample : OddList String := .cons \"x\" (.cons \"y\" (.cons \"z\" .nil))\nexample : OddList String := .cons \"x\" (.cons \"y\" .nil)\nUnknown constant `OddList.nil`\n\nNote: Inferred this name from the expected resulting type of `.nil`:\n  OddList String\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Type System\u0009Inductive Types","header":"4.4.5. Mutual Inductive Types","id":"/The-Type-System/Inductive-Types/#mutual-inductive-types"},"/Type-Classes/Instance-Synthesis/#instance-synth":{"contents":"Instance synthesis is a recursive search procedure that either finds an instance for a given type class or fails.\nIn other words, given a type that is registered as a type class, instance synthesis attempts to construct a term with said type.\nIt respects reducibility: semireducible or irreducible definitions are not unfolded, so instances for a definition are not automatically treated as instances for its unfolding unless it is reducible.\nThere may be multiple possible instances for a given class; in this case, declared priorities and order of declaration are used as tiebreakers, in that order, with more recent instances taking precedence over earlier ones with the same priority.\n\nThis search procedure is efficient in the presence of diamonds and does not loop indefinitely when there are cycles.\nDiamonds occur when there is more than one route to a given goal, and cycles are situations when two instances each could be solved if the other were solved.\nDiamonds occur regularly in practice when encoding mathematical concepts using type classes, and Lean's coercion feature  naturally leads to cycles, e.g. between finite sets and finite multisets.\n\nInstance synthesis can be tested using the #synth command.\nAdditionally, inferInstance and inferInstanceAs can be used to synthesize an instance in a position where the instance itself is needed.\ninferInstance with a type annotation and inferInstanceAs are not equivalent; inferInstanceAs preprocesses the synthesized instance to prevent unintentional leakage of implementation details into interfaces.\n\ninferInstance synthesizes a value of any target type by typeclass\ninference. This function has the same type signature as the identity\nfunction, but the square brackets on the [i : α] argument means that it will\nattempt to construct this argument by typeclass inference. (This will fail if\nα is not a class.) Example:#check (inferInstance : Inhabited Nat) -- Inhabited Nat\n\ndef foo : Inhabited (Nat × Nat) :=\n  inferInstance\n\nexample : foo.default = (default, default) :=\n  rfl\n\n\ninferInstanceAs α synthesizes an instance of type α and then adjusts it to conform to the\nexpected type β, which must be inferable from context.Example:def D := Nat\ninstance : Inhabited D := inferInstanceAs (Inhabited Nat)\nThe adjustment will make sure that when the resulting instance will not \"leak\" the RHS Nat when\nreduced at transparency levels below semireducible, i.e. where D would not be unfolded either,\npreventing \"defeq abuse\".More specifically, given the \"source type\" (the argument) and \"target type\" (the expected type),\ninferInstanceAs synthesizes an instance for the source type and then unfolds and rewraps its\ncomponents (fields, nested instances) as necessary to make them compatible with the target type. The\nindividual steps are represented by the following options, which all default to enabled and can be\ndisabled to help with porting:* backward.inferInstanceAs.wrap: master switch for instance adjustment in both inferInstanceAs\nand the default deriving handler* backward.inferInstanceAs.wrap.reuseSubInstances: reuse existing instances for the target type\nfor sub-instance fields to avoid non-defeq instance diamonds* backward.inferInstanceAs.wrap.instances: wrap non-reducible instances in auxiliary definitions* backward.inferInstanceAs.wrap.data: wrap data fields in auxiliary definitions (proof fields are\nalways wrapped)If you just need to synthesize an instance without transporting between types, use inferInstance\ninstead, potentially with a type annotation for the expected type.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Type Classes","header":"10.3. Instance Synthesis","id":"/Type-Classes/Instance-Synthesis/#instance-synth"},"/ValidatingProofs/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Validating-a-Lean-Proof--The-Blue-Double-Check-Marks--Comments":{"contents":"In the Visual Studio Code extension settings, the symbol can be changed.\nEditors other than VS Code may have a different indication.\n\nRunning build +Module, where Module refers to the file containing the theorem, and observing success without error messages or warnings provides the same guarantees.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Validating a Lean Proof\u0009The Blue Double Check Marks","header":"Comments","id":"/ValidatingProofs/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Validating-a-Lean-Proof--The-Blue-Double-Check-Marks--Comments"},"/platforms/#platforms":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference","header":"Supported Platforms","id":"/platforms/#platforms"},"/releases/v4.19.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___19___0-_LPAR_2025-05-01_RPAR_--Other":{"contents":"* #7326 updates the release notes script to better indent PR\ndescriptions.* #7453 adds \"(kernel)\" to the message for the kernel-level application\ntype mismatch error.* #7769 fixes a number of bugs in the release automation scripts, adds a\nscript to merge tags into remote stable branches, and makes the main\nrelease_checklist.py script give suggestions to call the\nmerge_remote.py and release_steps.py scripts when needed.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.19.0 (2025-05-01)","header":"Other","id":"/releases/v4.19.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___19___0-_LPAR_2025-05-01_RPAR_--Other"},"/releases/v4.27.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___27___0-_LPAR_2026-01-24_RPAR_--Other":{"contents":"* #11328 fixes freeing memory accidentally retained for each document\nversion in the language server on certain elaboration workloads. The\nissue must have existed since 4.18.0.* #11437 adds recording functionality such that shake can more\nprecisely track whether an import should be preserved solely for its\nattribute commands.* #11496 implements new flags and annotations for shake for use in\nMathlib:Options:\n--keep-implied\nPreserves existing imports that are implied by other imports and thus\nnot technically needed\nanymore--keep-prefix\nIf an import X would be replaced in favor of a more specific import\nX.Y... it implies,\npreserves the original import instead. More generally, prefers\ninserting import X even if it\nwas not part of the original imports as long as it was in the original\ntransitive import closure\nof the current module.--keep-public\nPreserves all public imports to avoid breaking changes for external\ndownstream modules--add-public\nAdds new imports as public if they have been in the original public\nclosure of that module.\nIn other words, public imports will not be removed from a module\nunless they are unused even\nin the private scope, and those that are removed will be re-added as\npublic in downstream\nmodules even if only needed in the private scope there. Unlike\n--keep-public, this may\nintroduce breaking changes but will still limit the number of inserted\nimports.Annotations:\nThe following annotations can be added to Lean files in order to\nconfigure the behavior of\nshake. Only the substring shake:  directly followed by a directive\nis checked for, so multiple\ndirectives can be mixed in one line such as -- shake: keep-downstream, shake: keep-all, and they\ncan be surrounded by arbitrary comments such as -- shake: keep (metaprogram output dependency).* module -- shake: keep-downstream:\nPreserves this module in all (current) downstream modules, adding new\nimports of it if needed.* module -- shake: keep-all:\nPreserves all existing imports in this module as is. New imports now\nneeded because of upstream\nchanges may still be added.* import X -- shake: keep:\nPreserves this specific import in the current module. The most common\nuse case is to preserve a\npublic import that will be needed in downstream modules to make sense\nof the output of a\nmetaprogram defined in this module. For example, if a tactic is\ndefined that may synthesize a\nreference to a theorem when run, there is no way for shake to detect\nthis by itself and the\nmodule of that theorem should be publicly imported and annotated with\nkeep in the tactic's\nmodule.public import X  -- shake: keep (metaprogram output dependency)\n\n...\n\nelab \\\"my_tactic\\\" : tactic => do\n... mkConst ``f -- f, defined in X, may appear in the output of\nthis tactic\n```* #11507 optimizes the filesystem accesses during importing for a ~3% win\non Linux, potentially more on other platforms.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.27.0 (2026-01-24)","header":"Other","id":"/releases/v4.27.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___27___0-_LPAR_2026-01-24_RPAR_--Other"},"/releases/v4.33.1/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___33___1-_LPAR_2026-08-21_RPAR_--Other":{"contents":"* #14833\nmakes Lean require GMP 6.3.0 or newer and builds the official releases against GMP 6.3.0. Earlier GMP versions contain bugs that can cause Lean to produce unsound (i.e., incorrect) results in corner cases; independent kernels that do not depend on GMP will catch such unsoundness. The portable Linux releases were previously linked against GMP 6.1.2 (inherited from the old glibc nixpkgs used for portability).* #14849\nmakes the kernel reject Nat literals and computations whose representation would exceed a configurable size limit (128 MB by default). This prevents pathological or adversarial inputs from driving the kernel to spend unbounded memory and time constructing enormous numerals, and keeps the kernel's arithmetic comfortably within the range where its arbitrary-precision integer backend is well exercised. The limit can be raised with the LEAN_NAT_MAX_SIZE environment variable for the rare workloads that legitimately compute very large numerals in the kernel.* #14847\nadds another test for the is_prop bug in the kernel.\nThe exploit was submitted by Daniel Selsam (OpenAI) and was generated using OpenAI's internal models.* #14843\napplies the fix from #14807 to inductive.h. As the comment in inductive.h points out, the code should check whether e_type is a proposition using is_prop, but it was still inlining the old, buggy version of is_prop.* #14808\nadds a new defensive check to the kernel. When the kernel generates a recursor for an inductive type, it installs the recursor and its computation rules with add_core, which does not re-check them. It adds a verification pass that (1) type-checks each generated recursor's type and (2) checks that each computation rule is type-preserving: reducing the recursor applied to a constructor yields a term whose type is the recursor's declared result type. This catches a recursor whose minor-premise type and reduction rule disagree, for example a minor premise that expects an induction hypothesis while the rule omits it. Checking only that a rule's right-hand side has some type is not enough, because an under-applied minor premise is still a well-typed (function) term. The check is defense-in-depth: it does not change what the kernel accepts for well-formed inductives, and only rejects declarations that were already malformed.* #14807\nfixes a soundness issue. The kernel's is_prop decided whether a term is a proposition by taking the weak head normal form of its inferred type and checking that the result is syntactically Sort 0. When the inferred type did not reduce to a sort but was left as a stuck term, is_prop returned false instead of treating the term as ill-formed. This let the proof-irrelevance guard in projection inference be skipped, so a non-proof field could be projected out of a value used as a Prop, and False derived. The fix computes the inferred type with ensure_sort, which reduces it and requires the result to be a sort, raising (kernel) type expected otherwise. The bogus proof was also accepted by nanoda, an independent implementation of the Lean kernel. We believe the lean4lean external kernel does not have this bug.* #14806\nfixes a soundness issue in the kernel. The kernel cached successful is_def_eq queries in a union-find structure. Because the implemented is_def_eq is sound but incomplete, and therefore not transitive, the transitive closure computed by the union-find made a query's result depend on the order of earlier queries: is_def_eq(v0, v2) could return false on its own but true after is_def_eq(v0, v1) and is_def_eq(v1, v2) had succeeded. A crafted input used this to build a recursor whose type and computation rule disagreed, and derive False. The fix replaces the union-find with a plain cache keyed on the query pair, so is_def_eq is again a function of its two arguments. The issue was reported by Daniel Selsam (OpenAI) using their internal models. An OpenAI agent then produced two distinct exploits based on it. Both exploits are also caught by nanoda, and both are caught by the new lean-inductive-models developed by Joachim Breitner.* #14161\nadds support for compiling with thread sanitizer. This both increases memory consumption and slows lean down massively so we only run a very small subset of tests to remain in a reasonable time. Developers need to add additional tests to the set themselves.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.33.1 (2026-08-21)","header":"Other","id":"/releases/v4.33.1/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___33___1-_LPAR_2026-08-21_RPAR_--Other"},"/releases/v4.36.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___36___0-rc2-_LPAR_2026-10-27_RPAR_--Tactics":{"contents":"* #15750\nfixes two grind regressions introduced by #15420 and reported in leanprover/cslib#1141, where proofs about inductive predicates over λ-terms that v4.35.0 proved fail on v4.36.0-rc1. The normalizer now rewrites ∀ _ : α, True to True for every α, not only for propositions, and the eager cases that grind applies to new hypotheses resets the E-matching round counter like any other case split.* #15678\nfixes grind failing to prove goals such as c = b → a ≤ b + c + d → a ≤ b + b + d in an ordered ring when the Sym.simp normalizer is enabled (set_option backward.grind.normalizer false). The normalizer rewrites b + b + d to 2 * b + d, so the two inequalities are no longer congruent after c = b, and no module related the two sums.* #15675\nfixes grind failing with the internal error \"structure does not have a lawful LT instance\" (or \"structure does not support LT\") when linarith has to reason about < on a type without a LawfulOrderLT instance. For example, [IntModule α] [LE α] [LT α] [IsLinearOrder α] [OrderedAdd α] (a b c : α) : a < b → b < c → a < c now fails with a regular grind failed message.* #15672\nfixes grind failing to use k⁻¹ for a numeral k in a field when the Sym.simp normalizer is enabled (set_option backward.grind.normalizer false). For example, [Field R] [IsCharP R 0] (h : f (a * 2⁻¹) = 1) (h2 : a = 2 * b) : f b = 1 was not proved.* #15642\nadds the option Grind.Config.distrib (default false) to the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer. By default, a product of two non-constant polynomials such as x * (y + 1), a power of a sum, and the inverse of a product are kept as atoms whose factors are normalized, as the legacy normalizer does; grind +distrib expands them into polynomial normal form. Linear arithmetic procedures treat nonlinear terms as atoms, so keeping them unexpanded preserves facts such as x * (y + 1) = x * z after y + 1 = z is derived.* #15624\nmakes grind put k#w literals with a symbolic width w in the OfNat.ofNat form it uses for BitVec literals, so with the Sym.simp-based normalizer theorems such as BitVec.sdiv_zero apply to x.sdiv 0 as they do with the legacy normalizer.* #15623\nmakes the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer rewrite dependent propositional binder domains, so ∀ h : i < a.size, q h becomes ∀ h : i + 1 ≤ a.size, ... as with the legacy normalizer.* #15619\nfixes a panic in the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer on match equations whose patterns contain arithmetic, e.g. grind [h.eq_def] for a function matching on Vec α (n + 1 + 1).* #15618\nfixes spurious ring term with unexpected instance issues in grind for casts over abstract rings, e.g. (m + n) • a = m • a + n • a with [CommRing R], and the corresponding failures of the Sym.simp-based normalizer.* #15616\nadds BitVec.zero, BitVec.ofNat _ (Bool.toNat _), and BitVec.carry to the list of supported terms by bv_decide* #15612\nfixes grind losing a hypothesis whose type is marked [grind cases eager] when the hypothesis is introduced by intros or asserted as a parameter fact. Only the constructor fields survived in each branch, so example : P → ¬P → False := by grind and grind [(mkP)] on the goal P failed for a predicate P with field-less constructors.* #15611\nfixes grind? failing with \"proof contains unresolved internal metavariable\" when a parameter fact has a type marked [grind cases eager]. Asserting such a fact splits the goal during initialization, and grind? only produced a script for the first goal. It now handles every goal, and the suggested script carries one · block per goal.* #15609\nmakes grind [mkP] accept a theorem whose statement is not a ∀ and is not indexable, e.g., theorem mkP : P for a nullary inductive predicate P. The parameter is asserted as a fact, so the workaround grind [(mkP)] is no longer needed. grind? and finish? keep such identifiers in their suggestions.* #15606\nfixes a spurious \"generated tactic cannot close the goal\" warning from finish? when its parameters alone close the goal, e.g. grind => finish? [inj f_inj].* #15602\nfixes grind? and finish? dropping parameters such as inj f_inj and funCC q from their suggestions, which then failed when pasted.* #15600\nfixes grind failing on instances wrapped in @[implicit_reducible] definitions.* #15599\nfixes a grind internal error, ring term has not been internalized, triggered when the ring solver learned a disequality such as a ≠ b while a term containing a - b was still being internalized. The Rabinowitsch step then built (a - b) * (a - b)⁻¹ and internalized it before (a - b)⁻¹ had an ENode. Closes #15590.* #15589\nfixes a panic in grind ring when propagating implied equalities in rings without NoNatZeroDivisors, such as BitVec n.* #15588\nfixes a grind internal error (ring term has not been internalized) triggered when a user-defined Pow Int Nat instance with higher priority is in scope. Mathlib's Monoid.npow instance on Int hits this case after #15506.* #15555\nmakes vcgen take the program type and the WP instance from the goal's wp application when it applies a spec. Thus vcgen works on a goal whose program is not of the instance's program type, for example a program e : Exp in a goal wp (Prog := Tagged n Exp) e Q E for def Tagged (n : Nat) (α : Type) := α.* #15582\nmakes cbv evaluate cond (bif c then a else b) lazily, like ite and dite. Before this change, cbv evaluated both branches of cond, even when the condition was the literal true. An expensive or non-terminating branch that the condition does not select cost time or exhausted cbv.maxSteps. The fix gives the cond simproc pattern all four arguments of cond.* #15497\nadds a guard to WP.Frames, so that vcgen can frame a resource across a program whose footprint depends on the state.* #15526\nfixes a kernel error when sym => simp rewrites inside a non-dependent arrow (e.g. x + 0 = y → p) in a file using module.* #15515\nmakes vcgen split a spec precondition fun _ => ∃ h : i < xs.size, Q () xs[i] into the VC i < xs.size and the entailment for Q () xs[i] under a hypothesis h, so that the wp of the program tail in the body is stepped. The precondition fun _ => ⨆ h : i < xs.size, Q () xs[i] splits the same way.* #15524\nfixes a grind internal error (unknown free variable) on hints such as grind [h _ _ pf] whose only hole is a proof. It broke List.pairwise_iff_forall_infix in Mathlib after #15506.* #15506\nfixes a grind internal error (semiring term has not been internalized) triggered when power propagation rebuilds a term whose arguments are still being internalized (#15479).* #15503\nfixes sym with arith and grind with the Sym.simp-based normalizer on goals whose equations are over a type in Sort u, such as {α : Sort u} (a b : α). The ring classifier computed the universe level of the carrier with getDecLevel, which fails with \"invalid universe level, u is not greater than 0\" for such types; it now uses getDecLevel? and reports that the type has no ring structure, as the order classifier already did.* #15491\nmakes vcgen recognize more @[spec] preconditions as conjunctive in the schematic postcondition Q/E. vcgen applies such a spec directly and runs no frame inference for it.* #15487\nmakes vcgen split a pointwise ∧ in a spec's precondition like a lattice meet ⊓.* #15483\nmakes Sym.simp rewrite a raw Nat literal that is not the argument of a numeral into the OfNat.ofNat form, as Meta.simp does. Rewrite rules whose pattern variable binds the raw literal of a numeral, such as the [grind hom] rule (OfNat.ofNat a : Fin n).val = a % n, produced terms like nat_lit 0 % (p + 1) that no later step recognized as a numeral, so grind with the Sym.simp-based normalizer failed on goals over Fin (p + 1) and BitVec n.* #15482\nmakes grind prove goals over UIntN and BitVec that multiply by a numeral close to the modulus, such as -1 * a or 4294967295 * a over UInt32; these previously exhausted the lia step budget. The [grind hom] injection into Nat turned (c * a).toNat into c * a.toNat % 2^w, and cutsat splits on the coefficient c when it has to eliminate a.toNat exactly, while (-a).toNat is (2^w - a.toNat) % 2^w, with coefficient 1.* #15478\nfixes a kernel-rejected proof in grind's ring module. For an inverse a⁻¹ with a numeral a, processInv asserts a * a⁻¹ = 1 or a⁻¹ = 0 with theorems whose numeral is denoteInt k, i.e. an OfNat numeral or its negation, but the recognizer also accepted ↑(0 : Nat), ↑(3 : Int), -0, and - -3, which are only propositionally equal to denoteInt k; Field.inv_zero : 0⁻¹ = 0 was then pushed as a proof of (↑0)⁻¹ = ↑0.* #15476\nmakes the Sym arithmetic normalizer rewrite a cast of a numeral (↑(0 : Nat), ↑(-2 : Int)) to the numeral of the carrier wherever it occurs, as the legacy grind normalizer does. Casts were interpreted only inside +, *, ...; a cast standing alone, as a function argument or under ⁻¹, was kept, so (↑0)⁻¹ reached the ring solver of grind, which applied Field.inv_zero to it and produced a proof rejected by the kernel (grind_9321 with backward.grind.normalizer := false).* #15475\nmakes the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer rewrite every Fin literal to its canonical form. A literal such as @OfNat.ofNat (Fin (1 + 1)) 0 Fin.instOfNat, with a nested numeral as value and a ground type index, was left as is, and grind treated it as a literal distinct from (0 : Fin 2), producing proofs rejected by the kernel (grind_canon_ofnat).* #15473\nadds a Sym.dsimp pass after Sym.simp in the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer, so that beta-redexes and projections of structure instances in the fixed arguments of an application are reduced, as the legacy normalizer does with Simp.Config.dsimp. Sym.simp does not visit these arguments, and a redex left in the implicit objects of a morphism composition made the goals of Functor.flip in grind_cat.lean miss NatTrans.naturality.* #15467\nmakes the projection reduction of Sym (Sym.reduceProjApp?, used by Sym.simp, Sym.dsimp, and the canonicalizer) expose the structure argument at .reducible transparency, as simp does, instead of the ambient one. With the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer, E-matching patterns are normalized at attribute time at .default transparency, so a pattern such as (asIso f).hom unfolded asIso and collapsed to a variable, and the theorem was rejected with invalid pattern.* #15466\nfixes two ways a grind congruence table entry could go stale, which made grind.debug fail nondeterministically on goals with match conditions, and adds a deterministic invariant check for the table.* #15465\nmakes the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer respect the annotations on match equations: Grind.simpMatchDiscrsOnly (only the discriminants of the match are simplified) and Grind.PreMatchCond (the condition is left as is). E-matching instances of match equations, e.g. from f.eq_def, now work with backward.grind.normalizer := false, where they used to fail or produce proofs rejected by the kernel.* #15464\nadds the option backward.grind.normalizer. It defaults to true, which selects the legacy simp-based grind normalizer; with false, grind normalizes hypotheses, the target, E-matching instances, generalized patterns, and E-matching patterns at attribute time with the Sym.simp-based normalizer developed with grind_norm. The default behavior is unchanged.* #15463\nextends Sym.Simp.evalGround to evaluate Int.toNat, Int.natAbs, and toNat on UInt8, UInt16, UInt32, and UInt64 literals, e.g. (-7 : Int).natAbs becomes 7. grind is affected too, since the [grind hom] rewriter uses evalGround.* #15461\nmakes the realized constants required by bv_decide private to avoid name clashes while\nimporting multiple modules that realized them.* #15460\nextends Sym.Simp.evalGround to evaluate String.push and String.singleton on literals, e.g. \"abc\".push 'd' becomes \"abcd\". grind is affected too, since the [grind hom] rewriter uses evalGround.* #15458\nmakes the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer produce bit-vector literals in OfNat.ofNat form, the form grind keeps, instead of BitVec.ofNat. Ground bit-vector terms such as 3#8 + 5#8, 3 &&& 5, (3#8).zeroExtend 16, and 300#8 are now evaluated to the same literals as the legacy normalizer.* #15448\nfixes a kernel error in grind on goals mentioning both Char.ofNat 97 and 'a', and makes grind evaluate Char.toNat, Char.val, and Char.ofNat when the argument is equal to a literal, e.g. y = 'a' → y.toNat = 97 and n = 97 → Char.ofNat n = 'a'.* #15445\nmakes grind prove goals about Fin.log2 and about the scoped Nat and Int casts into Fin n through their val interpretation, such as (x.log2).val = Nat.log2 x.val, ((i : Int) : Fin n).val = (i % n).toNat, and ((k : Nat) : Fin n).val = k % n.* #15434\nmakes the Sym.Arith normalizer solve equations over commutative rings with a nonzero characteristic, such as Fin n, UInt8, and BitVec w, when it runs in the mode used by the grind normalizer. An equation k * m + k' = 0 or k * m₁ = k * m₂ whose coefficient k is invertible modulo the characteristic is solved for the monomial: in Fin 5, 4 * x + 2 = 0 becomes x = 2, as 2 * x = 6 already becomes x = 3 over Int.* #15433\nextends Sym.Simp.evalGround to evaluate the Fin functions on literals: succ, castSucc, rev, last, val, pred, castAdd, addNat, natAdd, castLT, castLE, subNat, mk, and ofNat. It also normalizes Fin literals: (7 : Fin 5) becomes 2, and a type index that is not a numeral, as in (4 : Fin (5 + 1)), is evaluated. Fin arithmetic and comparisons were already supported.* #15398\nintroduces a CEGAR/Lemmas on Demand procedure into bv_decide for supporting uninterpreted functions even without interacting with grind mode. Towards this it refactors the solving pipeline to be fully incremental and makes use of incremental SAT solving. The congruence feature is available behind bv_decide +uf, it is currently limited to:1. Only functions that return Bool/BitVec2. Only performing congruence reasoning on non-dependent Bool/BitVec arguments (see the bv_uf test for examples of what this entails)* #15420\nfixes a grind regression introduced by #15378: a goal such as Imp (∀ i, i < n → ∃ j, j = i + 1 ∧ p j) (p 4), where Imp a b unfolds to a → b through a [grind norm] rule, was no longer proved, while the same goal written with a syntactic → still was. grind now normalizes the goal before negating it, and the normalizer no longer rewrites (∀ x, p x) → q into (∃ x, ¬p x) ∨ q.* #15419\nextends Sym.Simp.evalGround to evaluate Char.val on character literals, e.g. 'a'.val becomes 97. It complements #15411, which added the other Char operations.* #15417\nfixes an Application type mismatch error in grind when the homomorphism rewriter translates an equality whose hypothesis mentions a let-bound literal, e.g. b = m.cpop with let m : BitVec 2 := 1#2. Closes #15388.* #15411\nextends Sym.Simp.evalGround to evaluate Char operations on character literals: toNat, toLower, toUpper, toString, isWhitespace, isUpper, isLower, isAlpha, isDigit, isAlphanum, ==, and !=. It also converts Char.ofNat n into a character literal when n is a numeral, so that a character has a single representation.* #15409\nadds Sym.Simp.reduceControl, a variant of simpControl that does not block the simplifier: it simplifies the condition of ite/dite/cond and the discriminants of match, reduces the term when they are decided, and otherwise lets the simplifier visit the branches. The Sym.simp-based grind normalizer uses it in place of reduceMatcher.* #15405\nfixes a sharing violation in the arith simproc of Sym.simp, e.g., on a + f (b + 0). With sym.debug enabled, it surfaced as a panic in assertShared.* #15322\nmakes Sym.intros introduce a binder whose name starts with __ as an implementation-detail local, as the elaborator does for such binders. vcgen introduces the join point __do_jp of a do block with Sym.intros, so its verification conditions no longer show the join point and its body in the local context.* #15389\nfixes a sharing violation in Sym.dsimp when it descends into a fun, ∀, or let binder whose bound variable occurs in the body or in a later binder type. With sym.debug enabled, the violation surfaced as a panic in assertShared.* #15383\nadds the fallback modifier to the [grind hom] attribute. A fallback rule is applied to a term only when no other homomorphism rule matches it, using the second tier Sym.simp already has for eq_def unfolding.* #15378\nfixes the pushNot simproc of the grind normalizer so that ¬(p → q) becomes p ∧ ¬q and ¬∀ x, p x becomes ∃ x, ¬p x. These rewrites have been dead code since #9271: the simproc matched the forallE case against the whole ¬p instead of p, and checked the domain with the syntactic Expr.isProp, which is false for a proposition variable. The same isProp check is fixed in the Sym.simp-based normalizer.* #15377\nremoves the grind = attribute from the lemmas that are already [grind hom] rules, so that grind derives the toNat, toInt and val image of a term once, through the homomorphism rewriter, instead of twice.* #15370\nmakes grind prove goals that mention the Bool-valued signed comparisons BitVec.slt and BitVec.sle, such as (x < y) ↔ (x.toBitVec.slt y.toBitVec = true) for Int64, x.slt y = true ↔ x.toInt < y.toInt, x.slt y → y.sle z → x.slt z and x.slt y = !(y.sle x).* #15369\nmakes grind prove goals about the signed value of a bitwise complement, such as (~~~x).toInt = (~~~x.toInt).bmod (2 ^ 16) for BitVec 16 and the corresponding goals for the fixed-width signed integer types, as well as plain Int facts such as ~~~a = -a - 1.* #15367\nmakes grind prove goals about the signed value of a bit vector shifted left by a variable amount, such as (x <<< n).toInt = (x.toInt <<< n).bmod (2 ^ 16), and lets nested signed results compose, such as ((x <<< m) <<< n).toInt = (x.toInt * 2 ^ m * 2 ^ n).bmod (2 ^ 16), ((x + y) <<< n).toInt = ((x.toInt + y.toInt) * 2 ^ n).bmod (2 ^ 16) and ((x * y) * z).toInt = (x.toInt * y.toInt * z.toInt).bmod (2 ^ 16).* #15366\nmakes simp [f] and dsimp [f] in sym => mode behave like the default simplifier when f is a definition or a conditional theorem. Side conditions of conditional theorems, such as the equational theorems of definitions with overlapping patterns, are now discharged using grind. Non-recursive definitions are unfolded when no equational theorem applies. dsimp accepts definitions and rfl-theorems. Reducible definitions are ignored with a warning instead of failing.* #15363\nfixes a grind failure on goals containing -0 : Int, such as f (-((0 : Nat) : Int)) = f 0, where grind produced a proof rejected by the kernel. -0 is now normalized to 0.* #15365\nmakes grind relate the unsigned value of an arithmetic shift to the signed value of its operand: goals such as ((x.sshiftRight n).toNat : Int) = (x.toInt / 2 ^ n) % 2 ^ 16 over BitVec 16 are now proved.* #15364\nmakes grind reason about bit-vector rotations through its [grind hom] injection into Nat: goals such as (x.rotateLeft n).toNat = (x.toNat * 2 ^ (n % 16) + x.toNat / 2 ^ (16 - n % 16)) % 2 ^ 16 over BitVec 16, and the rotateRight counterpart, are now proved.* #15362\nfixes Sym.DSimp.zetaDelta and Sym.DSimp.zetaDeltaAll so that they unfold a let-bound variable that appears as the head of an application. Previously, dsimp [foo] and dsimp [*] in grind/sym, and the bv_decide normalization passes, left terms such as foo a b untouched when foo := fun x y => x + y was a local definition, while Meta.DSimp reduces them to a + b.* #15350\nmakes grind reason about bit-vector exponentiation through its [grind hom] injections: goals such as (x.pow z).toNat = x.toNat ^ z % 2 ^ 16 and (x.pow z).toInt = (x.toInt ^ z).bmod (2 ^ 16) over BitVec 16 are now proved.* #15348\nmakes grind reason about bit-vector concatenation through its [grind hom] injection into Nat: goals such as (x ++ y).toNat = x.toNat * 2 ^ 8 + y.toNat over BitVec 8 are now proved. It also fixes Sym.simp rules whose left-hand side has Nat arithmetic in a type index, such as the width m + n of (x ++ y).toNat, which never fired on terms whose width had been normalized to a numeral.* #15347\nlets the cutsat solver in grind reorder its variables more than once during a proof search. Variables created after the first model search, e.g. by later E-matching rounds, were appended to the variable order; when such a variable has a large coefficient in its bound constraints, the Cooper case split that eliminates it enumerated that coefficient and exhausted liaSteps. For example, (x <<< 1).toInt = (x.toInt <<< 1).bmod (2 ^ 16) over BitVec 16 failed because the quotient x.toNat / 2 ^ 16, introduced by the instance of BitVec.toInt_eq_toNat_bmod, ended up last in the order with a coefficient of 2 ^ 16. Such goals are now proved.* #15345\nadds support for &&& with a constant mask to grind, for masks of the form 1…10…0 (a run of ones followed by a run of zeros), over Nat and, through the [grind hom] injections, BitVec and the fixed-width integer types. The mask is rewritten into %, /, and * by literals, which cutsat supports: x &&& (2^n - 1) becomes x % 2^n, and x &&& ((2^n - 1) * 2^k) becomes x / 2^k % 2^n * 2^k. For example, (x &&& 0xe0).toNat = x.toNat / 32 % 8 * 32 over BitVec 8 and x &&& 0xC0 = 0 → x &&& 0x40 = 0 over UInt8 are now proved by grind. Masks with holes (e.g. 0b101) and Int are not handled yet.* #15344\nmakes Sym.Arith.normalize? (and hence the arith simproc of Sym.simp) cancel x * x⁻¹ for an atom x when a discharger proves x ≠ 0, in any field. a * b / a becomes b, a ^ 3 / a becomes a ^ 2, and a / (2 * a) becomes 2⁻¹. The simproc DSL accepts arith with d, so arith with grind uses the goal's hypotheses. A result that asked for a side condition is context-dependent.* #15343\nmakes numeral inverses rational coefficients in Sym.Arith.normalize? (and hence in the arith simproc of Sym.simp) for fields of characteristic zero. a / 2 + a / 2 now normalizes to a, a / 2 + b / 3 to (3 * a + 2 * b) * 6⁻¹, and relations become denominator-free: a / 2 + b / 3 = 1 becomes 3 * a + 2 * b = 6. Without an IsCharP _ 0 instance, numeral inverses remain atoms.* #15259\nregisters BitVec.toNat_ofInt as a [grind hom] rule, so grind can reason about the unsigned value of BitVec.ofInt. In particular, cutsat can now follow carries computed through BitVec.ofInt.* #15262\nregisters BitVec.toInt_signExtend as a [grind hom] rule, so grind can reason about the signed value of BitVec.signExtend: narrowing reduces toInt modulo 2 ^ v, and widening leaves it unchanged.* #15311\nfixes grind never activating E-matching theorems whose ground pattern contains a lambda, a dependent ∀, or a nonparametric literal such as a Char or a negative Int. For example, @[grind .] theorem myPred_of_true : myPred (fun _ => True) was never used even when the goal contained myPred mySet and mySet = fun _ => True, and @[grind =] theorem fc_a : fc 'a' = 1 could not prove fc 'a' = 1. Passing such theorems explicitly with grind [myPred_of_true] worked, since explicit parameters bypass symbol indexing.* #15310\nfixes Sym.dsimp producing terms with dangling free variables when a binder type depends on an earlier binder, e.g. ∀ k : Nat, k < 32 → .... This caused bv_decide to fail with unknown free variable. Closes #15195.* #15046\nfixes Lean.Meta.Sym.Simp.toHave, which passed the dependencies of a have to Expr.betaRev in forward order even though betaRev expects them reversed. Any have whose value depended on two or more earlier haves of the same telescope was rebuilt with its dependencies swapped, producing an ill-typed proof term that the kernel rejects with an application type mismatch.* #14827\nmakes vcgen state exception postcondition verification conditions pointwise for more exception postcondition types, and makes throws clauses work for custom types. For Except ε, a spec with the exception postcondition fun e => e = \"x\" now yields ∀ e, e = \"x\" → … in place of an entailment between two functions. A custom type such as structure Thrown where onThrow : String → Prop gets the same pointwise verification conditions from a ToEStack instance, which converts it to an exception postcondition stack. With an EPostSlot instance, the type also supports throws clauses of def contracts.* #15290\nmoves the syntax of the vcgen tactic to the new module Std.WP.Tactic and deprecates the tactics of the Std.Do proof mode. import Std.WP now provides vcgen. Each call of a proof mode tactic, such as mintro or mspec, reports one deprecation warning that points to vcgen. The option mvcgen.warning is deprecated because it has no effect.* #15298\nteaches Sym.Arith.normalize? (and hence the arith simproc of Sym.simp) the field rewrites that need no side conditions. In a Field, a / b becomes a * b⁻¹, and inverses are pushed down to the atoms: (a * b)⁻¹ ↦ a⁻¹ * b⁻¹, (-a)⁻¹ ↦ -a⁻¹, a⁻¹⁻¹ ↦ a, 0⁻¹ ↦ 0, 1⁻¹ ↦ 1, after normalizing the term under the inverse. x⁻¹ for an atom or numeral x is then an atom of the polynomial, so a / b + a / b normalizes to 2 * (a * b⁻¹).* #15297\nextends Sym.Arith.normalize? (and hence the arith simproc of Sym.simp) to non-commutative Ring and Semiring structures. Monomials keep the order of their factors, so a * b and b * a stay distinct and (a + b)^2 normalizes to a^2 + a*b + b*a + b^2. Terms, relations, and the characteristic are supported as for the commutative case.* #15296\nfixes the arith simproc of Sym.simp so that reflexive relations between atoms are closed: x ≤ x and x = x now normalize to 0 ≤ 0 and 0 = 0 and are closed by post, like x + 0 ≤ x already was.* #15295\nmakes the relation normalizer of Sym.Arith (and hence the arith simproc of Sym.simp) use the characteristic of the ring, so that 256 * u + v = v on UInt8 normalizes to 0 = 0 and u + 2 = v is recognized as already normal. Previously only terms were reduced modulo the characteristic.* #15293\nextends Sym.Arith.normalize? (and hence the arith simproc of Sym.simp) to relations lhs = rhs, lhs ≤ rhs, and lhs < rhs over a CommRing or CommSemiring. Everything is moved to one side and split by sign, so x + y = z + 2 * x becomes y = x + z, and a ≤ b + a becomes 0 ≤ b; when both sides normalize to the same polynomial, the goal is closed.* #15292\nadds the arith simproc to Sym.simp, which normalizes ring and semiring terms into polynomial normal form using Sym.Arith.normalize?. It is opt-in through register_sym_simp and intended as a pre simproc: it sees the maximal arithmetic subtree first, simplifies its atoms with the full simplifier, and reifies it once. Normal forms are final (done := true) and cached.* #15280\nmakes Expr.toPolyS and Expr.toPolyS_nc handle a zero exponent like Expr.toPoly does, so x ^ 0 in a semiring normalizes to 1 instead of a monomial with exponent 0. The Sym.Arith normalizer now rewrites x ^ 0 : Nat to 1.* #15279\nadds Sym.Arith.normalize?, which rewrites a term of a CommRing or CommSemiring into polynomial normal form with a proof by reflection. It is the normalizer that Sym.simp will use for arithmetic; this PR provides the SymM-level API and its tests, not the simplifier integration.* #15267\nfixes regressions in bv_decide around projection and match reduction.* #15263\nmoves the classification of order structures (LE, IsPreorder, LT, LawfulOrderLT, IsPartialOrder, IsLinearPreorder, OrderedRing) from grind's order module into Sym.Arith, so other Sym components can reuse it. No user-visible change.* #15190\nmakes the grind ring solver use the ring classification, operator caches, reification, and denotation from Sym.Arith, and deletes the copies it kept under Grind/Arith/CommRing. A type is now classified once per SymM run instead of once per goal, so every goal of a grind run and every tactic of a sym block share the result. Code that drives grind programmatically sees the new layout: the per-goal ring state holds solver data only, Result carries the Sym.Arith ring records, and goalToMessageData and evalCheck take them.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.36.0-rc2 (2026-10-27)","header":"Tactics","id":"/releases/v4.36.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___36___0-rc2-_LPAR_2026-10-27_RPAR_--Tactics"}});